        ],
        "grammar": [
          {
            "sentence": "El temor es que muchos de esos volúmenes acaben destruidos durante el proceso, porque suele cortarse el lomo para pasar las páginas por el escáner.",
            "point": "Subjunctive after an expression of fear",
            "explanation": "After 'el temor es que' the verb moves into the subjunctive, so 'acabar' becomes 'acaben'. The same thing happens after 'temo que', 'me preocupa que' or 'es posible que'.",
            "why_tricky": "In English you simply say 'the fear is that many end up destroyed', with no change to the verb, so learners keep the indicative and write 'acaban'."
          },
          {
            "sentence": "Sin embargo, quienes defienden el patrimonio advierten de que se están perdiendo ediciones difíciles de reemplazar.",
            "point": "'quienes' as a free relative pronoun, plus the passive with 'se'",
            "explanation": "'Quienes' means 'those who' and needs no antecedent in front of it. 'Se están perdiendo ediciones' is a passive built with 'se': the editions are being lost and no agent is named.",
            "why_tricky": "Learners default to 'los que' everywhere, and they translate the passive literally as 'son perdidas', which sounds heavy in Spanish."
