            "example": "Quedó a la sombra de su hermano mayor.",
            "tier": "B2"
          },
          {
            "front": "reivindicar",
            "pos": "verb",
            "back": "to claim, to assert the value of something",
            "example": "El museo reivindica el trabajo de las mujeres científicas.",
            "tier": "C1"
          },
          {
            "front": "el papel",
            "pos": "noun",
            "back": "role",
            "example": "Tuvo un papel clave en las negociaciones.",
            "tier": "B1"
          },
          {
            "front": "el intérprete",
            "pos": "noun",
            "back": "interpreter (person who translates speech)",
            "example": "Necesitamos un intérprete para la reunión.",
            "tier": "B1"
          },
          {
            "front": "protagonizar",
            "pos": "verb",
            "back": "to be the main actor in something, to lead it",
            "example": "Los estudiantes protagonizaron la protesta.",
            "tier": "B2"
          },
          {
            "front": "el libro de texto",
            "pos": "noun",
            "back": "textbook",
            "example": "Ese dato no aparece en el libro de texto.",
            "tier": "B1"
          }
        ],
        "grammar": [
          {
            "sentence": "Se le conoce como Estevanico y formaba parte de una expedición que naufragó frente a la costa de Florida.",
            "point": "Impersonal 'se' with the pronoun 'le', plus imperfect versus preterite",
            "explanation": "'Se le conoce' means 'he is known', with no stated subject. Then 'formaba' describes a lasting situation and 'naufragó' reports a single completed event.",
            "why_tricky": "Learners write 'él es conocido', which is much less natural, and they often pick the wrong past tense for background description."
          },
          {
            "sentence": "De los cientos de hombres que habían salido de España, solo cuatro sobrevivieron.",
            "point": "Pluperfect for an event earlier than another past event",
            "explanation": "'Habían salido' places the departure before the moment being narrated. The main event, 'sobrevivieron', stays in the preterite.",
            "why_tricky": "Learners flatten everything into the preterite and lose the sense of one past action coming before another."
          },
          {
            "sentence": "Su historia recuerda que la conquista no la protagonizaron solamente los capitanes cuyos nombres aparecen en los libros de texto.",
            "point": "The relative 'cuyo' and the doubled object pronoun",
            "explanation": "'Cuyos' means 'whose' and agrees with the noun that follows it, 'nombres', not with the owner. The extra 'la' repeats the object 'la conquista' because it has been moved to the front of the clause.",
            "why_tricky": "Learners try 'de quien' instead of 'cuyo', and they drop the doubled pronoun that native speakers keep when the object comes first."
          }
        ],
